WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:04.430
Ever since the Marvel superheroes made the
jump from the comic book page to the silver

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:08.770
screen, there’s been one thing that ties
them together more than anything else: the

00:00:08.770 --> 00:00:10.730
cameo appearances by Stan Lee.

00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:14.580
But while comic fans have been spotting the
co-creator of the Marvel Universe show up

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:19.170
on screen for decades, not every cameo has
been as good as the others.

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:23.690
Here’s every Marvel movie cameo by Stan
the Man, ranked from worst to best.

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:25.460
Excelsior, True Believers!

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:27.240
And rest in peace, Stan Lee.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:29.460
We'll miss seeing you on the big screen.

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:30.460
Man-Thing

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:33.700
While audiences always look forward to seeing
Stan show up in Marvel movies, you can’t

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:37.340
really blame him for skipping out on this
Sci-Fi Channel original film that was shot

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:38.840
on the cheap in Australia.

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:43.000
That’s an awfully long flight for Captain
America, let alone Man-Thing.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:47.760
Instead, Stan only shows up as a photo as
a missing person, and even then he’s nearly

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:49.670
impossible to spot.

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:54.110
That said, it’s pretty hilarious to imagine
Stan having to fight off a swamp monster,

00:00:54.110 --> 00:00:55.370
even if we don’t get to see it.

00:00:55.370 --> 00:00:56.370
X-Men: The Last Stand

00:00:56.370 --> 00:00:59.800
While you might think Stan's cameos are a
mandatory part of anything that hits theaters

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:03.460
with a Marvel logo attached to it, that's
not exactly the case.

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:07.470
He never appeared in the Blade trilogy, and
only showed up in about half of the movies

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:08.470
in the X-Men franchise.

00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:13.540
It’s bad luck, then, that he missed out
on great mutant-themed movies like Logan and

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:16.110
X-Men: First Class instead of skipping out
on this one.

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:21.380
The cameo itself, in which he marvels at a
garden hose defying gravity, is pretty basic,

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:26.061
and it's nice that Stan can be spotted alongside
legendary X-Men writer Chris Claremont, who

00:01:26.061 --> 00:01:29.800
spent 17 years as the primary writer of those
comics.

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:35.000
Unfortunately, they're both surrounded by
a film that could charitably be called "disappointing."

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:38.619
Turns out that garden hose was actually more
interesting than anything else going on in

00:01:38.619 --> 00:01:39.619
that movie.

00:01:39.619 --> 00:01:40.619
X-Men

00:01:40.619 --> 00:01:44.550
The very first Stan Lee cameo of the modern
era of Marvel movies is mostly interesting

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:46.759
for showing us how far they’ve come.

00:01:46.759 --> 00:01:51.280
These days, Stan’s cameos are an event unto
themselves, but in 2000?

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:55.770
The co-creator of the X-Men was a hot dog
vendor with no dialogue, in the background

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:59.280
of a crowd scene whose brief appearance was
pretty easy to miss.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:00.280
Avengers

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:05.030
The Avengers was a movie tasked making every
piece of the MCU up to that point work together,

00:02:05.030 --> 00:02:09.049
so it's pretty understandable that most of
its runtime was focused on trying to do that.

00:02:09.049 --> 00:02:13.249
It's not until after the climax, when the
Avengers have come together as a team to save

00:02:13.249 --> 00:02:17.901
the world from an invading army of Chitauri,
that Lee finally makes his appearance in a

00:02:17.901 --> 00:02:18.980
news broadcast.

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:21.010
"Superheroes in NY?

00:02:21.010 --> 00:02:23.049
Gimme a break."

00:02:23.049 --> 00:02:27.099
It's an easy joke, but it works thanks entirely
to Lee’s effortless charm.

00:02:27.099 --> 00:02:28.239
Iron Man 3

00:02:28.239 --> 00:02:32.829
One of the recurring gags about Stan's cameos
in the MCU movies is that he's often portrayed

00:02:32.829 --> 00:02:34.239
as a dirty old man.

00:02:34.239 --> 00:02:37.680
In the otherwise underrated Iron Man 3, that
lasciviousness hits critical mass when we

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:42.040
see him judging a beauty pageant and getting
pretty worked up over the contestants.

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:45.739
In the end, not even Stan was happy with how
his cameo turned out.

00:02:45.739 --> 00:02:51.739
"My cameo had a couple of scenes in it, that
were funny has hell!"

00:02:51.739 --> 00:02:55.769
Unfortunately, Stan's cameo was cut for time,
leaving everyone unhappy.

00:02:55.769 --> 00:02:58.189
Congratulations anyhow, Miss Elk Ridge!

00:02:58.189 --> 00:03:03.109
"It's not my fault really I had nothing to
do with it!

00:03:03.109 --> 00:03:04.109
That's it!"

00:03:04.109 --> 00:03:05.109
Hulk

00:03:05.109 --> 00:03:09.040
Stan's first speaking cameo in a Marvel film
came in Ang Lee's much-maligned Hulk movie,

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:14.180
in which he showed up alongside former Hulk
actor Lou Ferrigno as a security guard.

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:16.099
That raises a lot of questions.

00:03:16.099 --> 00:03:20.349
Why would you hire an 81 year-old man to be
a security guard?

00:03:20.349 --> 00:03:21.349
Shouldn't he have retired?

00:03:21.349 --> 00:03:25.579
Does the Stan Lee of this movie's universe
need money so badly that he's willing to take

00:03:25.579 --> 00:03:27.359
a potentially dangerous job?

00:03:27.359 --> 00:03:31.819
Look, we're not saying Stan shouldn't be in
the movie, but maybe if that lab had hired

00:03:31.819 --> 00:03:35.920
someone else to work security, there wouldn't
be so many gamma-ray monsters running around.

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:36.999
Amazing Spider-Man 2

00:03:36.999 --> 00:03:41.290
Stan's appearance in this movie was built
entirely around the gag of having Stan Lee

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:43.060
show up and say that he knows Spider-Man.

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:46.549
It’s almost clever, but doesn't quite work.

00:03:46.549 --> 00:03:50.879
On one hand, Stan Lee should know Spider-Man
he co-created the character and wrote over

00:03:50.879 --> 00:03:52.989
a hundred issues of his comic book.

00:03:52.989 --> 00:03:56.790
On the other hand, if he's just talking about
Peter Parker within the world of the movie,

00:03:56.790 --> 00:04:01.049
he should still know who he is he was the
librarian at his high school in the previous

00:04:01.049 --> 00:04:02.299
movie.

00:04:02.299 --> 00:04:03.299
Guardians of the Galaxy

00:04:03.299 --> 00:04:05.870
Guardians of the Galaxy features one of the
only Stan Lee appearances where the other

00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:08.029
characters actually talk about him.

00:04:08.029 --> 00:04:12.329
In this case, he's a victim of Rocket's running
commentary on how much he hates the humanoid

00:04:12.329 --> 00:04:16.449
population of the planet Xandar, identifying
him as…

00:04:16.449 --> 00:04:19.109
"A class-A pre-vert."

00:04:19.109 --> 00:04:22.090
Pretty harsh, but maybe Rocket saw Iron Man
3.

00:04:22.090 --> 00:04:23.090
Venom

00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:27.819
In a movie that otherwise embraces Spider-Man's
most goopy foe in all his glory, Stan's cameo

00:04:27.819 --> 00:04:31.360
right before the credits roll in Venom is
pretty disappointing.

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:35.620
Viewers spent the entire runtime waiting to
see if their favorite mustachioed nonagenarian

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:40.900
makes an appearance, only to encounter a scene
so unnecessary that it could've been cut without

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:42.430
anyone ever noticing.

00:04:42.430 --> 00:04:46.800
Sure, it's fun that this random dude on the
street knows that Eddie Brock has a slimy

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:52.180
alien space cannibal living under his skin,
but after seeing the same idea done better

00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:53.930
elsewhere, it's a letdown.

00:04:53.930 --> 00:04:58.139
Is it too much to ask to see Stan the Man
with giant knives for fingers?

00:04:58.139 --> 00:04:59.139
Spider-Man

00:04:59.139 --> 00:05:03.139
With the first Spider-Man movie, director
Sam Raimi gave us a scene where Stan actually

00:05:03.139 --> 00:05:08.139
got to be a hero himself, saving a kid from
danger when the Green Goblin attacks.

00:05:08.139 --> 00:05:11.841
Not a bad tribute to the guy who wrote the
first hundred issues of Spidey's comic, but

00:05:11.841 --> 00:05:16.430
also nowhere near the fun that Raimi’s long-time
collaborator Bruce Campbell got to have in

00:05:16.430 --> 00:05:19.189
his far-more-compelling Spider-Man cameos.

00:05:19.189 --> 00:05:20.189
Spider-Man 2

00:05:20.189 --> 00:05:24.270
Stan's cameo in the second Spider-Man film
is almost exactly the same as it was the last

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:28.729
time around: a heroic passer-by who pulls
someone out of danger during Spidey's battle

00:05:28.729 --> 00:05:29.969
with a supervillain.

00:05:29.969 --> 00:05:33.229
The only thing that distinguishes it from
the first one — and puts it above it in

00:05:33.229 --> 00:05:37.469
our rankings — is what's going on around
it: Spider-Man's first big battle against

00:05:37.469 --> 00:05:42.520
Doctor Octopus, which starts in a bank and
winds up scaling high-rise walls in the middle

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:43.520
of Manhattan.

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:46.420
It’s a great scene, even if Stan’s appearance
feels like a re-run.

00:05:46.420 --> 00:05:47.580
Daredevil

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:52.660
2003's Daredevil movie was memorable for all
the wrong reasons, but Stan Lee's appearance

00:05:52.660 --> 00:05:54.590
was one of its few nice touches.

00:05:54.590 --> 00:05:59.289
In a nod to Daredevil's comic book origin,
a young Matt Murdock keeps a distracted Lee

00:05:59.289 --> 00:06:02.740
from stepping out in front of a truck, despite
his blindness.

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:06.669
Of course, that doesn't explain why they changed
Daredevil's origin to take out the part where

00:06:06.669 --> 00:06:11.370
he's blinded because he's saving someone,
but hey, at least Stan fares better than one

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:13.770
of the movie's other creator cameos.

00:06:13.770 --> 00:06:17.610
Legendary Daredevil writer/artist Frank Miller
showed up and got stabbed in the head with

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:18.610
a pen.

00:06:18.610 --> 00:06:19.610
Thor: The Dark World

00:06:19.610 --> 00:06:23.639
The second Thor movie goes with another easy
gag, having Lee pop up as a patient in a mental

00:06:23.639 --> 00:06:25.430
institution for a quick laugh.

00:06:25.430 --> 00:06:28.610
"Can I have my shoe back?"

00:06:28.610 --> 00:06:31.080
As for how he got in there, we’ve got a
theory.

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:35.689
Maybe this version of Stan is the one who
remembers all the other movies he's made cameos

00:06:35.689 --> 00:06:37.979
in that weren't part of the MCU.

00:06:37.979 --> 00:06:42.219
It's easy to imagine him being dragged off
while ranting about the X-Men and the Fantastic

00:06:42.219 --> 00:06:46.580
Four, or wondering why nobody in this universe
seems to cuss as much as they did when he

00:06:46.580 --> 00:06:48.340
was hanging out with Deadpool.

00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:52.120
Seeing three different Spider-Men in less
than a decade is enough to send anybody to

00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:53.120
therapy.

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:54.129
Spider-Man: Homecoming

00:06:54.129 --> 00:06:57.479
Homecoming’s scenes of Spider-Man patrolling
Manhattan and helping out average New Yorkers

00:06:57.479 --> 00:06:59.460
are some of the film’s finest moments.

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:04.460
It shows two important parts of Spidey's personality:
he's willing to help everyone with everything,

00:07:04.460 --> 00:07:07.800
and he's not immune to screwing up on a pretty
regular basis.

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:11.789
It's that second part that leads to Lee's
appearance, as an entire neighborhood gets

00:07:11.789 --> 00:07:15.370
together to yell at our wall-crawling hero
for making too much noise in the middle of

00:07:15.370 --> 00:07:19.300
the day… and also bouncing some poor guy's
head off his own car just for locking his

00:07:19.300 --> 00:07:20.310
own keys inside.

00:07:20.310 --> 00:07:23.270
"Don’t make me come down there, you punk!”

00:07:23.270 --> 00:07:26.639
It’s pretty appropriate to have Stan scolding
Spidey, too.

00:07:26.639 --> 00:07:29.289
When you think about it, he really is kind
of Spider-Man's dad.

00:07:29.289 --> 00:07:33.060
Which, it turns out, is nowhere near as dangerous
as being his uncle.

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:34.060
Ant-Man and the Wasp

00:07:34.060 --> 00:07:38.590
Stan Lee's best cameos work with the pretty
simple idea of dropping the comics' lovable

00:07:38.590 --> 00:07:42.640
grandpa into weird situations, and Ant-Man
and the Wasp is no different.

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:48.509
"The '60s were fun, but now I'm paying for
it."

00:07:48.509 --> 00:07:53.740
An older fellow in the famous Haight-Ashbury
district would probably have had a very specific

00:07:53.740 --> 00:07:55.580
kind of fun back then.

00:07:55.580 --> 00:08:00.939
In our world, however, we know exactly what
Lee was doing in the '60s: inventing weird,

00:08:00.939 --> 00:08:05.310
increasingly improbable situations involving
superheroes who could shrink to the size of

00:08:05.310 --> 00:08:06.310
ants.

00:08:06.310 --> 00:08:08.040
Trippy enough, even without the hippie help.

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:09.040
Captain America: The First Avenger

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:13.509
If you were going to show someone exactly
one Stan Lee cameo to explain what he actually

00:08:13.509 --> 00:08:15.870
does in these movies, this would be the one.

00:08:15.870 --> 00:08:20.610
It’s the kind of joke that could’ve been
done by anyone, but Lee being there elevates

00:08:20.610 --> 00:08:25.180
it, and makes it a little more than just an
exercise in spotting Lee in a costume.

00:08:25.180 --> 00:08:27.379
"I thought he’d be taller!”

00:08:27.379 --> 00:08:28.379
Deadpool

00:08:28.379 --> 00:08:32.240
As we saw in Guardians of the Galaxy, there
seem to be few things that superhero filmmakers

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:36.450
enjoy more than casting Stan Lee as a dirty
old man — and he seems to get a kick out

00:08:36.450 --> 00:08:37.450
of it too.

00:08:37.450 --> 00:08:42.000
The R-rated Deadpool, unsurprisingly takes
that to a whole new level with Stan taking

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:43.760
the stage as a DJ.

00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:44.920
Trial of the Incredible Hulk

00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:50.000
While it wasn't on the big screen, Stan's
earliest Marvel movie cameo came in 1989's

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:51.160
Trial of the Incredible Hulk.

00:08:51.160 --> 00:08:56.850
A TV movie that aired on NBC, it was not only
a sequel to the Incredible Hulk series, it

00:08:56.850 --> 00:09:00.820
was also a backdoor pilot for a Daredevil
show that never happened.

00:09:00.820 --> 00:09:04.769
Stan's appearance this time is as a jury foreman
present when Banner Hulks out in the middle

00:09:04.769 --> 00:09:05.920
of a trial.

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:09.920
His main role here is to just look surprised,
but he winds up coming off a lot better than

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:13.331
some of the other jurors — a guy sitting
behind him in this scene decides that it's

00:09:13.331 --> 00:09:15.579
a good idea to throw his chair at the Hulk.

00:09:15.579 --> 00:09:16.579
Dude.

00:09:16.579 --> 00:09:17.620
What are you doing?

00:09:17.620 --> 00:09:18.620
Thor

00:09:18.620 --> 00:09:22.920
If his cameo in The First Avenger is the archetype,
Stan's appearance in Thor refines it into

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:24.000
something even better.

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:28.550
The idea of a bunch of people gathering around
a crater to drink beer and have a cookout

00:09:28.550 --> 00:09:32.639
while they all try to pick up a magic hammer
is great all on its own..

00:09:32.639 --> 00:09:36.750
Stan showing up as the guy who tears his own
pickup truck apart trying to drag it that

00:09:36.750 --> 00:09:37.980
hammer out?

00:09:37.980 --> 00:09:43.180
That's great, and even goes to show that the
Odinpower is even stronger than a Chevy Silverado.

00:09:43.180 --> 00:09:45.570
"Did it work?"

00:09:45.570 --> 00:09:47.180
Spider-Man 3

00:09:47.180 --> 00:09:51.310
After the simplicity of Stan's cameos in the
first two Spider-Man movies, his appearance

00:09:51.310 --> 00:09:53.650
in the third seems weirdly drawn out.

00:09:53.650 --> 00:09:57.121
While it was nice to have him in a speaking
role for the first time in the franchise,

00:09:57.121 --> 00:10:00.520
the long pause in his line feels strange."

00:10:00.660 --> 00:10:07.199
"You know, I guess one person can make a difference... Nuff said.”

00:10:07.199 --> 00:10:09.290
There is one little twist, though.

00:10:09.290 --> 00:10:13.389
Since it's easy to believe that Stan's playing
the same character in all three Spider-Man

00:10:13.389 --> 00:10:17.950
movies, that also means this is a guy who
previously saved two people's lives during

00:10:17.950 --> 00:10:19.540
a supervillain attack.

00:10:19.540 --> 00:10:22.860
Could that "one person” who made the difference
be…

00:10:22.860 --> 00:10:23.860
Stan Lee himself?

00:10:23.860 --> 00:10:24.860
Avengers: Age of Ultron

00:10:24.860 --> 00:10:29.199
Sure, Age of Ultron may be kind of a mess,
but the party scene where we get to see the

00:10:29.199 --> 00:10:33.949
Avengers just hanging out and having fun is
probably the highlight of the movie—especially

00:10:33.949 --> 00:10:38.420
since we also get to see Stan show up and
get trashed on thousand year-old Asgardian

00:10:38.420 --> 00:10:39.639
ale.

00:10:39.639 --> 00:10:40.870
"Eggshellcior!"

00:10:40.870 --> 00:10:43.329
Iron Man

00:10:43.329 --> 00:10:45.620
Forget about Thanos and the Infinity Stones.

00:10:45.620 --> 00:10:51.050
The biggest payoff in the entire MCU comes
from Stan Lee's interactions with Tony Stark.

00:10:51.050 --> 00:10:55.899
In their first encounter, Tony mistakes him
for Playboy publisher and smoking jacket enthusiast

00:10:55.899 --> 00:11:01.110
Hugh Hefner, and takes the first step towards
a pretty amazing payoff.

00:11:01.110 --> 00:11:02.120
Iron Man 2

00:11:02.120 --> 00:11:06.120
The second time that Stan meets Tony Stark,
he's mistaken for Larry King.

00:11:06.120 --> 00:11:10.980
And look, no disrespect, but that's actually
pretty insulting to Stan Lee, who has managed

00:11:10.980 --> 00:11:14.250
to stay spry and wiry well into his 90s.

00:11:14.250 --> 00:11:15.250
Ant-Man

00:11:15.250 --> 00:11:18.700
Lee's cameo in Ant-Man comes as part of a
genuinely great scene that gets some great

00:11:18.700 --> 00:11:22.380
comedy out of Luis and his winding, fast-paced
storytelling.

00:11:22.380 --> 00:11:26.920
As great as this bit is, though, it stings
that we're robbed of hearing Stan Lee actually

00:11:26.920 --> 00:11:28.590
say his line here…

00:11:28.590 --> 00:11:30.410
"Yeah, CRAZY stupid fine!”

00:11:30.410 --> 00:11:31.410
Captain America: The Winter Soldier

00:11:31.410 --> 00:11:35.690
If there's one word that describes Lee's character
in these movies, it's "hapless."

00:11:35.690 --> 00:11:39.300
There's a sort of instant sympathy you have
for this guy after seeing him tear up his

00:11:39.300 --> 00:11:44.269
pickup truck or get clowned on by Tony Stark,
but in Winter Soldier, you really start to

00:11:44.269 --> 00:11:45.620
feel bad for him.

00:11:50.980 --> 00:11:52.150
This poor guy!

00:11:52.150 --> 00:11:53.150
C'mon, Cap!

00:11:53.150 --> 00:11:56.060
You couldn't just leave a note to help out
the security guards?

00:11:56.060 --> 00:11:57.060
Doctor Strange

00:11:57.060 --> 00:12:01.600
In what might be his most high-brow cameo
to date, Lee appears as a bus passenger getting

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:06.360
a kick out of a book, completely oblivious
to the mind-bending, reality-altering battle

00:12:06.360 --> 00:12:07.660
going on around him.

00:12:07.660 --> 00:12:08.660
The joke?

00:12:08.660 --> 00:12:13.850
Lee's book is a copy of The Doors of Perception,
a memoir detailing Aldous Huxley’s experience

00:12:13.850 --> 00:12:17.089
of experimenting with the hallucinogenic chemical
mescaline.

00:12:17.089 --> 00:12:22.000
It's a strange bit of commentary on the trippy
visuals of the film, but let's be real here:

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:26.790
if Huxley had seen New York folding in on
itself and an angular man in a Cloak of Levitation

00:12:26.790 --> 00:12:31.690
casting magic spells while he was tripping
on peyote , he probably wouldn't have been

00:12:31.690 --> 00:12:32.690
surprised.

00:12:32.690 --> 00:12:33.690
Thor: Ragnarok

00:12:33.690 --> 00:12:37.630
In Thor: Ragnarok, Stan makes an appearance
as the barber who cuts Thor's hair to get

00:12:37.630 --> 00:12:39.360
him ready for his fight with the Hulk.

00:12:39.360 --> 00:12:44.259
"Now don't you move, my hands aren't as steady
as they used to be."

00:12:44.259 --> 00:12:48.480
Not only does he provide a great straight-man
for Chris Hemsworth's hilarious blubbering

00:12:48.480 --> 00:12:53.690
about his precious golden locks, it also makes
perfect sense for someone looking for game-changing

00:12:53.690 --> 00:12:58.570
heroes and villains to be part of the Grandmaster’s
gladiatorial games — and it gives Stan his

00:12:58.570 --> 00:13:01.140
best costume ever.

00:13:01.140 --> 00:13:02.140
The Incredible Hulk

00:13:02.140 --> 00:13:06.579
The Incredible Hulk is usually the least-loved
entry in the MCU, but its use of Lee is one

00:13:06.579 --> 00:13:07.579
of the best.

00:13:07.579 --> 00:13:12.880
The film’s first major point involves Bruce
Banner's gamma-irradiated blood accidentally

00:13:12.880 --> 00:13:17.440
getting into a soda while he's working at
a bottling plant, temporarily turning anyone

00:13:17.440 --> 00:13:19.450
who drinks it into a Hulk.

00:13:19.450 --> 00:13:23.800
And naturally, the one poor hulked-out consumer
that we see is Stan Lee.

00:13:23.800 --> 00:13:28.839
So basically, this movie gave us the idea
of Stan Lee with Incredible Hulk Powers.

00:13:28.839 --> 00:13:32.800
The only downside is that we never actually
saw it, which might actually be the biggest

00:13:32.800 --> 00:13:34.670
oversight in the entire franchise.

00:13:34.670 --> 00:13:36.040
Avengers: Infinity War

00:13:36.040 --> 00:13:40.710
Stan’s cameo as Peter Parker's school bus
driver in Infinity War isn’t just good,

00:13:40.710 --> 00:13:41.740
it’s smart.

00:13:41.740 --> 00:13:43.991
"What's the matter with you kids?

00:13:43.991 --> 00:13:46.620
You’ve never seen a spaceship before?"

00:13:46.620 --> 00:13:50.399
Getting it out of the way early in the film
ensures that audiences would be paying attention

00:13:50.399 --> 00:13:55.480
to the epic interplanetary battle against
Thanos, rather than keeping an eye out for

00:13:55.480 --> 00:13:57.879
everyone’s favorite comic book grandpa.

00:13:57.879 --> 00:14:02.510
Not a bad idea, considering how much there
is to keep track of when every major hero

00:14:02.510 --> 00:14:05.600
in the MCU is slugging it out onscreen at
once.

00:14:05.600 --> 00:14:07.920
Uh, no offense, Ant-Man and Hawkeye.

00:14:07.920 --> 00:14:08.990
"Ant man?"

00:14:08.990 --> 00:14:11.000
"Yeah, you haven't heard of me?"

00:14:11.000 --> 00:14:16.690
As for what makes it good, it’s absolutely
Stan’s grumpy, way-too-calm evaluation of

00:14:16.690 --> 00:14:18.790
the Black Order’s invasion of Earth.

00:14:18.790 --> 00:14:22.930
If anyone’s seen enough spaceships that
it’s not even a big deal anymore, it’s

00:14:22.930 --> 00:14:23.930
Stan.

00:14:23.930 --> 00:14:26.740
He’s been dealing with aliens invading New
York since 1961.

00:14:26.740 --> 00:14:27.860
X-Men: Apocalypse

00:14:27.860 --> 00:14:32.829
In a sharp contrast to the blurry hot dog
vendor 16 years earlier, X-Men: Apocalypse

00:14:32.829 --> 00:14:37.440
gave viewers a tight close-up on Stan, using
the reflection of his signature sunglasses

00:14:37.440 --> 00:14:39.800
to show missiles streaking through the sky.

00:14:39.800 --> 00:14:44.220
It's a cool look, and it actually uses Stan's
physical presence in an interesting visual

00:14:44.220 --> 00:14:47.380
way, which is more than you can say for most
of his appearances.

00:14:47.380 --> 00:14:51.110
Behind the scenes, though, this is one of
the most memorable Lee cameos for a much more

00:14:51.110 --> 00:14:52.570
bittersweet reason.

00:14:52.570 --> 00:14:57.269
It marks the only time that Stan appeared
in a film alongside Joan, his wife of nearly

00:14:57.269 --> 00:14:58.269
60 years.

00:14:58.269 --> 00:15:01.000
Sadly, she'd pass away just over a year later.

00:15:01.000 --> 00:15:02.000
Fantastic Four

00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:07.040
Stan’s cameo in 2005's Fantastic Four marks
a huge departure from his usual roles.

00:15:07.040 --> 00:15:11.660
For the first time, Stan was playing a Marvel
character that he actually co-created: Willie

00:15:11.660 --> 00:15:14.389
Lumpkin, the Fantastic Four's mailman.

00:15:14.389 --> 00:15:18.079
That might not seem like a big deal, but believe
it or not, Willie has been around for some

00:15:18.079 --> 00:15:21.709
of the FF's most pivotal moments, and even
dated Aunt May.

00:15:21.709 --> 00:15:26.459
As a cheerful regular guy with a connection
to the entire Marvel Universe, it's the perfect

00:15:26.459 --> 00:15:27.529
role for Stan.

00:15:27.529 --> 00:15:28.529
Big Hero 6

00:15:28.529 --> 00:15:32.620
It's almost easy to forget that Big Hero 6
is a Marvel movie — at least until Stan

00:15:32.620 --> 00:15:34.170
Lee shows up at the end.

00:15:34.170 --> 00:15:39.009
It's a weird cameo, though, since Lee has
virtually no connection to these characters.

00:15:39.009 --> 00:15:44.860
Big Hero 6 was created in 1998, when Stan
was actually briefly out of his Marvel contract.

00:15:44.860 --> 00:15:49.500
At the same time, in a movie where the characters
struggle with older brothers and various other

00:15:49.500 --> 00:15:53.410
surrogate father figures, it's nice to know
that at least one of them has a father that

00:15:53.410 --> 00:15:54.410
we already like.

00:15:54.410 --> 00:15:55.410
Captain America: Civil War

00:15:55.410 --> 00:15:57.910
And here’s the big Iron man payoff.

00:15:57.910 --> 00:16:02.410
After getting snubbed twice by a man who keeps
mistaking him for others, Stan rolls up to

00:16:02.410 --> 00:16:06.149
the Avengers compound and delivers his own
tiny vengeance on Tony Stark.

00:16:06.149 --> 00:16:09.670
"Are you Tony Stank?"

00:16:09.670 --> 00:16:14.041
Since Tony Stark is unquestionably the most
famous person in the entire world, there's

00:16:14.041 --> 00:16:17.000
no way stan wouldn't be able to recognize
him.

00:16:17.000 --> 00:16:21.740
"Tony Stank" is revenge, served cold by a
man who's tired of being mistaken for Hugh

00:16:21.740 --> 00:16:23.940
Hefner and Larry King.

00:16:23.940 --> 00:16:24.940
Black Panther

00:16:24.940 --> 00:16:27.949
Black Panther boasts one of the most memorable
cameos Stan has ever done.

00:16:27.949 --> 00:16:33.180
It happens in the secret casino in South Korea,
an incredibly tense moment where T’Challa

00:16:33.180 --> 00:16:38.029
is at way-past-Bond levels of cool, and three
different groups are getting ready to collide

00:16:38.029 --> 00:16:39.710
in a massive fight scene.

00:16:39.710 --> 00:16:44.769
And then... here comes Stan, casually pocketing
the Panther’s casino winnings.

00:16:44.769 --> 00:16:46.430
Not like he needs ‘em, right?

00:16:46.430 --> 00:16:47.870
"You know what…

00:16:47.870 --> 00:16:51.800
I think I'll just take these, and bring them
over here.

00:16:51.800 --> 00:16:53.730
And hold on for safe-keeping."

00:16:53.730 --> 00:16:54.730
Amazing Spider-Man

00:16:54.730 --> 00:16:58.399
Having a wacky action going on in the background
while characters are blissfully unaware of

00:16:58.399 --> 00:17:02.850
it is a well-worn gag, but when Stan shows
up as an oblivious librarian during one of

00:17:02.850 --> 00:17:06.630
Amazing Spider-Man’s biggest fights, it's
done perfectly.

00:17:06.630 --> 00:17:11.000
What makes it even better is that after a
dozen years of cameos, this one puts Stan

00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:12.360
front and center for moviegoers.

00:17:12.360 --> 00:17:13.360
Teen Titans Go!

00:17:13.360 --> 00:17:14.360
To the Movies

00:17:14.360 --> 00:17:15.480
Stan's appearance in Teen Titans Go!

00:17:15.480 --> 00:17:19.400
To the Movies might be the single wildest
cameo he's ever made.

00:17:19.400 --> 00:17:23.840
This is, after all, a movie about superhero
movies, and Stan's appearance is based on

00:17:23.840 --> 00:17:28.720
the idea that the audience is familiar enough
with the tropes of big-screen capes and cowls

00:17:28.720 --> 00:17:31.380
to catch all the rapid-fire references.

00:17:31.380 --> 00:17:35.450
It's funny enough when Stan shows up as a
janitor in the Warner Bros. back lot.

00:17:35.450 --> 00:17:39.820
It's downright hilarious when he charges the
camera under a neon sign claiming that it's

00:17:39.820 --> 00:17:42.720
time for another one of his "subtle cameos."

00:17:42.720 --> 00:17:47.100
The punchline of his horrorstruck face when
he realizes he's accidentally showing up in

00:17:47.100 --> 00:17:49.910
a DC movie is just the icing on the cake.

00:17:49.910 --> 00:17:51.960
Except that it's not really the punchline.

00:17:51.960 --> 00:17:56.680
That comes later, when he crashes back into
the movie and assures us all that he doesn't

00:17:56.680 --> 00:17:57.680
care what movie he's in.

00:17:57.680 --> 00:17:58.680
"I'm back!

00:17:58.680 --> 00:18:00.680
I don’t care if it’s a DC movie.

00:18:01.880 --> 00:18:03.600
I love Cameos!

00:18:03.600 --> 00:18:05.540
Not now Stan Lee!

00:18:05.760 --> 00:18:06.760
"Excelsior!"

00:18:06.760 --> 00:18:07.540
Fantastic Four: Rise of the Silver Surfer

00:18:07.550 --> 00:18:12.520
For a movie that isn't remembered all that
fondly, 2007's Fantastic Four sequel managed

00:18:12.520 --> 00:18:15.330
to include one of the best Stan Lee cameos.

00:18:15.330 --> 00:18:18.460
It happens during the wedding of Reed Richards
and Sue Storm.

00:18:18.460 --> 00:18:20.250
"Invitation sir."

00:18:21.250 --> 00:18:22.440
"I should be on that list."

00:18:22.440 --> 00:18:23.440
"Name?"

00:18:23.440 --> 00:18:24.440
"Stan Lee."

00:18:24.440 --> 00:18:25.920
"Yeah, Nice try buddy."

00:18:25.920 --> 00:18:26.920
No NO!

00:18:26.920 --> 00:18:27.920
Really"

00:18:27.920 --> 00:18:32.140
Not only is this the one Marvel movie in which
Stan smashes through the fourth wall by actually

00:18:32.140 --> 00:18:36.660
identifying himself, it's a callback to a
similar scene one of his greatest comics of

00:18:36.660 --> 00:18:42.140
all time, Fantastic Four Annual #3… where
he's rejected from attending Reed Richards

00:18:42.140 --> 00:18:43.140
and Sue Storm's wedding.

00:18:43.140 --> 00:18:44.150
Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2

00:18:44.150 --> 00:18:47.860
Throughout the entirety of the Marvel Cinematic
Universe, there's been a question about whether

00:18:47.860 --> 00:18:52.190
this was a world that just happened to be
populated by a bunch of people who look exactly

00:18:52.190 --> 00:18:56.260
like Stan Lee, or if his cameos meant something…
more.

00:18:56.260 --> 00:19:01.960
In Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2, we finally
found out, and it was everything that a fan-theorist

00:19:01.960 --> 00:19:03.260
could've wanted.

00:19:03.260 --> 00:19:08.290
While Rocket, Groot, Yondu, and Kraglin are
careening through the galaxy on a series of

00:19:08.290 --> 00:19:13.670
warps, we find Stan, in the depths of space,
having a conversation with three of the Watchers,

00:19:13.670 --> 00:19:18.650
the mysterious beings tasked with chronicling
the history of a universe without ever interfering

00:19:18.650 --> 00:19:19.650
themselves.

00:19:19.650 --> 00:19:25.150
"Anyway… before I was so rudely interrupted
That time I was a Federal Express man."

00:19:25.150 --> 00:19:29.650
It becomes clear that all of these cameos
really are supposed to be the same guy.

00:19:29.650 --> 00:19:35.800
In the MCU, Stan Lee might not be a Watcher
himself, but he is their most reliable informant.

00:19:35.800 --> 00:19:39.530
Which explains why he's always around for
the universe's most important events.

